2026-02-10 11:58:30,876 - DocumentGraderNode - INFO: Retrieved document When trading index futures such as those of the S&P 500, traders may buy a futures contract, agreeing to purchase shares in the index at a set price six months from now. If the index goes up, the value of the futures contract will increase, and they can sell the contract at a profit before the expiration date. Selling futures works the other way around. If traders believe a specific equity is due for a fall and sell a futures contract, and the market declines as expected, traders can buy back the contract at a lower price, profiting from the difference. is relevant
